# Map And Reduce

## Overview

Map and Reduce work on ordered lists:

- `map` runs one Action for each item. Its result is a list of Action results
  in input order.
- `reduce` passes each item and an accumulator through one Action, one item at
  a time. Its result is the final accumulator.

Run the cells from top to bottom.

## Install The Package

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_action, "~> 3.0.0-beta.22"}])
```

## Define Item Actions

```elixir
defmodule FlowCollections.Double do
  use Jido.Action, name: "flow_collections_double"

  @impl true
  def run(%{value: value, index: index}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{value: value * 2, index: index}}
  end
end

defmodule FlowCollections.Sum do
  use Jido.Action, name: "flow_collections_sum"

  @impl true
  def run(%{total: total, value: value}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{total: total + value}}
  end
end
```

## Map Then Reduce

```elixir
defmodule FlowCollections.Example do
  use Jido.Flow, name: "flow_collections_example"

  flow do
    map "doubled",
      collection: input(:values),
      action: FlowCollections.Double,
      params: %{
        value: item(),
        index: item_index()
      }

    reduce "total" do
      collection result("doubled")
      initial %{total: 0}
      action FlowCollections.Sum

      params %{
        total: accumulator(:total),
        value: item(:value)
      }
    end

    output %{items: result("doubled"), total: result("total")}
  end
end
```

![Map doubles items in input order, then Reduce adds their values to a serial accumulator](assets/flows/map-reduce.svg)

The side arrow shows that the output also reads the complete Map result.
Map can run item calls concurrently; Reduce passes each accumulator to the
next call. The values shown use the input `[1, 2, 3]`.

```elixir
{:ok, %{items: items, total: %{total: 12}} = result} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowCollections.Example, %{values: [1, 2, 3]})

[%{value: 2, index: 0}, %{value: 4, index: 1}, %{value: 6, index: 2}] = items

result
```

`result("doubled")` reads the complete Map result: an ordered list of
`FlowCollections.Double` results. The Reduce reads that list without
consuming it, so the Flow output can return both results.

## Local References

| Reference | Map `params` | Reduce `params` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `item(path)` | The current item. | The current item. |
| `item_index()` | The zero-based index. | The zero-based index. |
| `item_id()` | A runtime identifier for this item. | A runtime identifier for this item. |
| `accumulator(path)` | Not available. | The current accumulator. |

An item ID is deterministic for the Flow, the component name, and the item
index. Each component uses its own name, so a Reduce over a Map result has
different item IDs than the Map. Do not use item IDs as execution IDs.

## Rules

- An empty Map returns `[]`. An empty Reduce returns its `initial` value.
- The Reduce `initial` value must be a map or a `Jido.Action.Output` value.
  A list or scalar compiles, but fails at runtime.
- Each Reduce call returns the next accumulator.
- The Map Action runs once per item, no matter how many components read the
  Map result.
- Map and Reduce targets must be Action modules, not Flow modules or inline
  bodies.

## Map Error Modes

With the default `on_error: :fail_fast`, the first item failure fails the Map.

With `on_error: :collect_errors`, an Action failure becomes a record instead
of failing the Map. A reference failure in `params`, such as a missing item
key, still fails the Map. The Map returns one record for each item, in input
order:

```text
%{status: :ok, value: action_result}
%{status: :error, error: %{type: type, message: message}}
```

`type` is the error module name as a string, such as
`"Elixir.Jido.Action.Error.ExecutionFailureError"`. `message` is the error
message. The record does not include error details or stacktraces.

```elixir
defmodule FlowCollections.Check do
  use Jido.Action, name: "flow_collections_check"

  @impl true
  def run(%{value: value}, _context) when value < 0 do
    {:error, Jido.Action.Error.execution_error("negative value")}
  end

  def run(%{value: value}, _context), do: {:ok, %{value: value}}
end

defmodule FlowCollections.CollectErrors do
  use Jido.Flow, name: "flow_collections_collect_errors"

  flow do
    map "checked",
      collection: input(:values),
      action: FlowCollections.Check,
      params: %{value: item()},
      on_error: :collect_errors

    output %{checked: result("checked")}
  end
end

{:ok, %{checked: checked}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowCollections.CollectErrors, %{values: [1, -2, 3]})

checked
```

Choose the mode on purpose. Jido does not add a fallback or retry for you.

## Concurrency

Map items can run concurrently when `max_concurrency` is greater than `1`:

```elixir
Jido.Exec.run(
  FlowCollections.Example,
  %{values: [1, 2, 3]},
  %{},
  max_concurrency: 2
)
```

The result order still matches the input order. Reduce always runs one item
at a time. See [Execution](execution.md) for runtime options.

## Data Form

In a map definition, use `kind: :map` with `collection`, `action`, `params`,
and `on_error`, or `kind: :reduce` with `collection`, `initial`, `action`, and
`params`. See [Flow Data Definitions](flow-data.md#reduce).
